\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Factors of the fixing subgroup}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{For the \vocabk{stabilizer}{fdip-0164} of a label #{r}, group
the axes first by their periods #{N} and then by the unordered
residue pairs #{\{v,-v\}} in #{\mathbb Z/N\mathbb Z}. Let #{I}
be any nonempty part, so that #{r_j\in\{v,-v\}} for #{j\in I}.
The stabilizer is the direct product of one factor per part. The
factor on #{I} is isomorphic to
##{\begin{cases}
\{1,-1\}^{I}\rtimes\operatorname{Sym}(I),&2v=0,\\
\operatorname{Sym}(I),&2v\ne0.
\end{cases}}
In the first group the permutations act on the signs by permuting
their coordinates.}

\proof{
\p{The fixing condition is #{s_jr_j=r_{\pi(j)}}.
It forces #{\pi} to preserve every part. If #{v=-v}, then every
coordinate in #{I} equals #{v}; each sign fixes that value, and all
permutations of #{I} are allowed. If #{v\ne-v}, any permutation of
#{I} determines the signs: #{s_j=1} when the two residues agree and
#{s_j=-1} when they differ. Forgetting the signs is a group
homomorphism, so in this case its bijection with the permutations
of #{I} is an isomorphism. Signed permutations on disjoint parts
commute. Restriction to the parts and assembly of their independent
actions are inverse maps, giving the product.}
}
